```gitignore
# Dependencies
node_modules/
**/node_modules/

# Build output
dist/
**/dist/
build/
**/build/

# Environment variables and secrets
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# Logs
logs/
*.log
npm-debug.log*
yarn-debug.log*
pnpm-debug.log*

# Cache
.cache/
.parcel-cache/
.eslintcache/

# Coverage
coverage/

# Operating system files
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
desktop.ini

# Editor files
.vscode/
.idea/

# Temporary files
tmp/
temp/

# Optional package manager files
# Keep package-lock.json, yarn.lock, and pnpm-lock.yaml in Git

### Apply `.gitignore` to your GitHub project

Run these commands in your terminal:

```bash
git add .gitignore
git rm -r --cached --ignore-unmatch node_modules dist build
git add .
git commit -m "Add gitignore and exclude dependencies and build files"
git push
```

**Important:** If your project has separate frontend and backend folders, this `.gitignore` will exclude `node_modules` and `dist` folders inside those folders too.

**Note:** If Git already tracks files inside nested `node_modules` or `dist` folders, the command above may not remove all of them from Git's tracking. The `.gitignore` will prevent new, untracked matching files from being added.